{
  "schemaVersion": "0.1.0",
  "kind": "chapter",
  "id": "chapter:partitioning-the-frankish-kingdoms",
  "editorial": {
    "status": "draft"
  },
  "sourceRefs": [
    {
      "sourceId": "source:gregory-histories-krusch-levison",
      "role": "supports",
      "locator": "II.43 and III.1. Clovis dies at Paris and is buried in the church of the Holy Apostles that he and Clotild had built. The four sons are named and their inheritance described as equal. III.1 gives no meeting place or list of seats."
    },
    {
      "sourceId": "source:gregory-histories-krusch-levison",
      "role": "supports",
      "locator": "III.6 and III.18. The pursuit at Visorontia, Godomar’s recovery of his kingdom, Clotild’s care for the boys and the fate of Chlodomer’s sons. The call imitating the king’s men is Gregory’s account of his death."
    },
    {
      "sourceId": "source:gregory-histories-krusch-levison",
      "role": "supports",
      "locator": "III.11. Chlothar and Childebert besiege Augustodunum. Godomar flees. Gregory links the siege with the acquisition of Burgundy."
    },
    {
      "sourceId": "source:gregory-histories-krusch-levison",
      "role": "supports",
      "locator": "IV.20–21. Childebert’s sickness and burial at Paris, Chlothar’s acquisition of the kingdom and treasure and his later burial by his four sons at Soissons."
    },
    {
      "sourceId": "source:gregory-histories-krusch-levison",
      "role": "supports",
      "locator": "IV.22, MGH SS rer. Merov. 1.1, pp. 154–155. Chilperic uses royal treasure and enters Paris. His brothers dislodge him. The narrative pairs each of the four kings with a predecessor and a sedes."
    },
    {
      "sourceId": "source:widdowson-merovingian-partitions",
      "role": "supports",
      "locator": "Abstract. Questions the degree to which Gregory’s pairing of 511 and 561 reflects formal settlements. His narrative served the political arguments of a later generation."
    },
    {
      "sourceId": "source:loseby-civitas-and-frankish-regions",
      "role": "supports",
      "locator": "pp. 223–225, 237–239 and nn. 64–67. Shares in cities and aristocratic loyalty in the Treaty of Andelot. Gregory’s use of Burgundy and Austrasia, absence of Neustria in his work, and the emergence of the three regional kingdoms."
    },
    {
      "sourceId": "source:isere-helmet-of-vezeronce",
      "role": "supports",
      "locator": "Écrans 3–7, printed pp. 19–22. Peat-bog find in 1870, battle of 524, the hypothetical identification with Chlodomer, gilding, six T-shaped bands and rivets."
    },
    {
      "sourceId": "source:beylie-vezeronce-grenoble-1909",
      "role": "derivation",
      "locator": "Plate captioned as the iron and bronze helmet found in 1870 at Vézeronce. Commons Summary gives the publication date, volume and Gallica leaf 225. Licensing records the Public Domain Mark."
    },
    {
      "sourceId": "source:wiseman-autun-and-burgundy-534",
      "role": "supports",
      "locator": "p. 23 and nn. 64–65. Marius of Avenches, Chronica s.a. 534 (MGH AA 11, p. 235), for the partition among Childebert, Chlothar and Theudebert. The author places the start of the siege in late 533 or early 534."
    },
    {
      "sourceId": "source:gregory-histories-brehaut-selections",
      "role": "supports",
      "locator": "IX.20, copy of the agreement at Andelot. Guntram retains the third part of Paris formerly held by Sigibert. Clauses on Cahors and Galswinth’s other cities."
    },
    {
      "sourceId": "source:wallace-hadrill-fredegar-fourth-book",
      "role": "supports",
      "locator": "IV.40–42, especially pp. 34–35. Frankish succession in 613, the desertion of Sigibert’s army, the capture and death of Brunhild, and the account of Chlothar II taking power over the kingdoms."
    },
    {
      "sourceId": "source:capitularia-chlothar-edict-bk9",
      "role": "supports",
      "locator": "BK 9, Date and Transmission. Editions by Pertz and Boretius date the edict to 18 October 614. Manuscript Berlin, Staatsbibliothek, Phill. 1743."
    },
    {
      "sourceId": "source:boretius-chlothar-edict-paris",
      "role": "supports",
      "locator": "No. 9, p. 22, c. 12, and p. 23, final subscription and dating clause. Judges from other provinces or regions, restitution from their own goods, and Paris as the place of issue."
    },
    {
      "sourceId": "source:connected-communities-vezeronce-282",
      "role": "supports",
      "locator": "Attestation 282. Gregory III.6, town identification, year 524 and named participants."
    },
    {
      "sourceId": "source:connected-communities-autun-292",
      "role": "supports",
      "locator": "Attestation 292. Gregory III.11, Latin and translation, siege of Autun and flight of Godomar. The record indexes the event under 534."
    },
    {
      "sourceId": "source:connected-communities-paris-399",
      "role": "supports",
      "locator": "Attestation 399. Gregory IV.20 for the death and burial of Childebert in Paris in 558."
    },
    {
      "sourceId": "source:connected-communities-paris-1173",
      "role": "supports",
      "locator": "Attestation 1173. Closing clause of the edict of Chlothar II, issue at Paris in 614."
    }
  ],
